<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marquis Wen of Wei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 445–396 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Marquess Wen of Wei, Wei Si</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marquis Wen of Wei was the founding ruler of the state of Wei, recognised as a state by the Zhou king in 403 BCE after the partition of Jin. He made Wei the leading power of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> through the legal reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-kui">Li Kui</a>, the irrigation works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ximen-bao">Ximen Bao</a> and the professional army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-qi">Wu Qi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marquis-wen-of-wei.jpg" alt="Marquis Wen of Wei" width="250" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown Uploaded by Philg88 at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WeiWenHou%E9%AD%8F%E6%96%87%E4%BE%AF.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Wei Si succeeded his father Viscount Huan as head of the Wei clan, one of the noble houses that dominated Jin, and in 424 BCE took the title of marquess on his own initiative. He sought out worthy men: the Confucian scholar Zixia came to Xihe at his invitation and trained students there, and he also consulted Tian Zifang and Duangan Mu. He appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-kui">Li Kui</a> to reform government on the principles that food requires labour and salary requires meritorious service, and Wei's laws were later codified as the Canon of Laws.</p><p>When Han and Zhao, at war with each other, each asked Wei for troops, he refused both, calling each ruler his brother, then mediated, and both came to pay tribute to Wei. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-qi">Wu Qi</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> territory east of the Luo River from 409 to 406 BCE, and its five captured cities became the Xihe Commandery. Ximen Bao, as magistrate of Ye, ended the yearly sacrifice of a maiden to the river god Hebo and cut twelve canals from the Zhang River, which turned its floods into irrigation.</p><p>Given passage through Zhao, Wei's army under Yue Yang besieged the capital of Zhongshan for three years and ended that state in 406 BCE. In 405 BCE an allied army of the three Jin houses under Zhai Jue attacked the Great Wall of Qi and captured Duke Kang of Qi. In 403 BCE King Weilie of Zhou recognised the leaders of Wei, Han and Zhao as rulers in their own right, an event often taken as the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On his deathbed in 396 BCE he entrusted his son, who succeeded as Marquess Wu of Wei, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-qi">Wu Qi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ximen-bao">Ximen Bao</a> and Beimen Ke. Sima Qian praised his eagerness to learn, and Sima Guang judged that Wei became the strongest of the Jin states. His appointment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-kui">Li Kui</a>, often regarded as the first Legalist, and of Zhai Huang, Yue Yang, Wu Qi and Ximen Bao arguably made Wei one of the first states to adopt measures later called Legalist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marquess Wen of Wei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Marquis Wen</b> led the family, and in 403 BCE the Zhou king recognised Wei as a state, with its capital at Anyi in southern Shanxi.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-wei">State of Wei</a></li></ul></section>
